WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.723
Why does a fragment bring back a whole memory?

2
00:00:06.603 --> 00:00:08.703
A few notes of a song, and the rest returns.

3
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:13.507
A rat runs a track while one hippocampal cell is recorded.

4
00:00:14.407 --> 00:00:16.207
Each spike drops a dot where the rat was.

5
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:21.743
Divide the floor into bins, and divide each bin's spikes by the time spent there.

6
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:25.887
One hot spot is a place field, and this is a place cell.

7
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:31.090
Each place cell fires in its own stretch, so a run is a sequence of cells.

8
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.220
One run engages a set of cells, firing in order.

9
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.630
Now store a pattern in four model units.

10
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:43.210
Agreeing units get a positive link, disagreeing units a negative one.

11
00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:46.263
Start from a cue with unit one wrong.

12
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:50.047
Each partner sends it a vote: link times state.

13
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:52.247
Which way will unit one go?

14
00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.680
All three votes say plus.

15
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.900
Unit one flips, and the stored pattern is back.

16
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.920
The energy counts violated links.

17
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.280
The cue violates three, and the stored pattern none.

18
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:16.560
Each flip removes more violation than it adds, so the state rolls downhill into a

19
00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.560
valley.

20
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.160
So here is the idea.

21
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.960
Learning strengthens links among cells active together.

22
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:28.173
Later, a fragment close enough to the stored pattern pulls the rest back on.

23
00:01:29.116 --> 00:01:32.176
In this model, recall is the group completing itself.

24
00:01:33.216 --> 00:01:38.736
The hippocampal region CA3 has dense recurrent connections, so it is modelled this

25
00:01:38.736 --> 00:01:39.736
way.

26
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.360
The idea has limits.

27
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.460
C A three's pyramidal-cell connections are excitatory, and in the brain, inhibition

28
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.640
helps regulate how many cells join in.

29
00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:55.983
In some models, a low threshold lets activity spread into other groups, and a high

30
00:01:55.983 --> 00:01:58.743
one leaves a weak cue unable to recruit its own.

31
00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:04.203
Feedback inhibition that tracks the total can keep activity sparse.

32
00:02:05.163 --> 00:02:09.443
With suitable connections and a good enough cue, a fragment can then complete its

33
00:02:09.443 --> 00:02:10.443
own pattern.

34
00:02:11.066 --> 00:02:13.366
Storing more patterns reshapes the landscape.

35
00:02:14.426 --> 00:02:16.346
Basins shrink, and false valleys appear.

36
00:02:17.206 --> 00:02:19.166
A poor cue can roll into the wrong one.

37
00:02:20.116 --> 00:02:26.376
Mice lacking NMDA receptors only in CA3 pyramidal cells learned a water maze

38
00:02:26.376 --> 00:02:27.376
normally.

39
00:02:28.816 --> 00:02:31.476
With all the room cues they recalled the platform.

40
00:02:32.376 --> 00:02:33.896
With few cues, they searched poorly.

41
00:02:34.966 --> 00:02:37.066
That is the deficit the model predicts.

42
00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:42.026
But completion was inferred from behaviour, not recorded in CA3.

43
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:47.550
In this model, recall is a group of linked cells completing itself from a part.
